/-
  OIBridge/BridgeOpDrive.lean — design module of the research thread `research/bridge`
  (round 5, node B20). Not certified. Built on a disposable dev branch only; not for merge.

  The sufficient bridge in the owner's form. Two token data, both INTRODUCED hypotheses, never
  discharged here:
    OpDrive(θ₀)  := ∀ n, TokenAvailAt 𝓘 arch zPh n   — K∞-Drive's operational reading at the 3-4-5
                     angle: the conjugation by `diag(1, w)`, `w = 3/5 + (4/5) i`, the operator of
                     `R_z(θ₀)`, available on the token alone at every level;
    the J-datum  := ∀ n, TokenAvailAt 𝓘 arch uJ n    — the conjugation by `(1 − i(X + Y + Z))/2`, the
                     operator of `J = cycEquiv`, available on the token alone at every level.
  Every other hypothesis is an inventory item of the stage-6 inventory: context stability (I4.29),
  label invariance (I4.30), the architecture (I4.32), unitary-ray saturation (I4.134); and the
  class's transfer clause (T)_𝓘 (`BridgeDictionary.TransferClause`), introduced. No step uses any
  availability beyond the two data: no phase at any angle, no exchange, no `diagonal_avail`, no
  `SubstratumAvail`, no `DrivesElementary`; no bind step. The kernel closure rules used: the
  spectator rule and the transport rule (token ⇒ pair, through `BridgeToken`), the transport and
  discard rules (pair ⇒ token), the ray step with the architecture's block clause (membership).

    §A  the definitions, and pair availability from token availability and conversely (B19);
    §B  membership: from token availability at a positive level, `c • U` lies in the class at `Fin 2`
        (the kernel's ray step `exists_scaled_mem_of_instAvail_unitary` and the block clause), and
        `U` itself under unitary-ray saturation;
    §C  the two transferred tables: `transferMap zPh ω = actT (rotZ (3/5) (4/5)) ω` and
        `transferMap uJ ω = actT cycEquiv ω` (the one dictionary's injectivity, `dict_actT_rotZ_345`
        and the equivalence thread's `dict_actT_cyc`);
    §D  the derived half: for a pair cone with the class's transfer clause, OpDrive(θ₀) gives the
        invariance under `actT R_z(θ₀)` and the J-datum the invariance under `actT J`
        (`bridge_derived_half`); at the kernel's own pair cone `Q3` both hold with no hypothesis
        (`Q3_two_invariant`);
    §E  refutations and controls: FM refutes both data; the monomial interface satisfies OpDrive(θ₀)
        and refutes the J-datum; the full class satisfies both; a pair cone with the transfer clause
        that is moved by `actT R_z(θ₀)` refutes OpDrive(θ₀) for that class (`not_opDrive_of_moved`).

  The bridge is SUFFICIENT, not necessary: nothing here excludes other token data (dense subgroups
  generated by finite-order data). The chain needs the EXACT availability of the two operations; a
  dense subgroup with a closure premise is a different statement, not built here.

  Kernel check (dev branch):  cd verification/lean-mathlib && lake exe cache get && lake build
-/
